/** @page oracleora_sandbox_connectionsguide Connections from Sandboxed Code

    @tableofcontents

    @section ora_sandbox_connections_intro Introduction

    When the connection values of a \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool were chosen by code under a sandbox that
    restricts network access, %Qore checks the destinations of each connection against the network policy of that
    sandbox before the driver connects.  The driver reports the destinations from the connect identifier, and a
    connection that the policy allows connects only to the numeric addresses that were checked: each \c ADDRESS of
    the connect descriptor is replaced by one \c ADDRESS for each checked address of its host, also when the
    connection is made again after it was lost.  A connection whose destinations the driver cannot determine exactly
    is refused with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED, and the message says why.

    Connections whose values were chosen by code that no such sandbox governs are made as before.

    @section ora_sandbox_connections_identifiers Connect Identifiers

    |!Connect identifier|!Destinations
    |a host and port set in the datasource|that host and port; the database name is the service
    |a connect descriptor: <tt>(DESCRIPTION=...)</tt> or <tt>(DESCRIPTION_LIST=...)</tt>|every \c ADDRESS, in order, also of failover and load-balancing lists; a missing \c PORT is \c 1521
    |an Easy Connect string: <tt>[tcp://]host[:port][,host[:port]...]/service[:server][/instance]</tt>|every host; a port applies to the hosts before it that have none, and the default is \c 1521; an IPv6 address is given in brackets
    |a TNS alias|the addresses of its connect descriptor in \c tnsnames.ora (see below)

    @section ora_sandbox_connections_aliases TNS Aliases

    A TNS alias is resolved from \c tnsnames.ora as the Oracle client resolves it, from every file that the client
    reads: \c ~/.tnsnames.ora, and \c tnsnames.ora in \c $TNS_ADMIN, in the \c network/admin directory of the Oracle
    client library (Instant Client) and in <tt>$ORACLE_HOME/network/admin</tt>, following \c IFILE entries.  It is
    refused:
    - if no such file defines it;
    - if two files define it differently;
    - if a \c sqlnet.ora file that the client reads uses another naming method before \c TNSNAMES
      (\c NAMES.DIRECTORY_PATH; LDAP, for example), or sets \c NAMES.DEFAULT_DOMAIN, with which the client would look
      up another name.

    @section ora_sandbox_connections_redirect Listener Redirects

    A listener can answer the client's connect request with a REDIRECT to another address, which the Oracle client
    follows while it attaches to the server, without a way to refuse it (a RAC SCAN listener redirects to the listener
    of a node, for example).  So after the attach, and before the login sends the credentials, the address that the
    client is actually connected to is checked against the network policy of the sandbox: a connection to an address
    that the policy allows proceeds (so a redirect within the allowed addresses works), and one to an address that it
    does not allow, or whose address cannot be determined, is detached and refused with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED.

    The address is read from the TCP sockets that the attach opened (Linux: \c /proc/self/fd and \c getpeername());
    a socket that another thread opened at the same time is checked too, which can refuse a connection but never
    allow one.  On other platforms, connections that a restricting sandbox chose are refused for now.

    The residual risk: before the check, the client has opened a TCP connection to each redirect target (a chain of
    redirects reaches each host of the chain) and sent it the connect request (the connect descriptor), but no
    credentials; a redirect to a TCPS address makes the TLS handshake there before the check, with the client's
    wallet certificate if one is configured; and only the direct peer is checked, so a redirect to an allowed relay
    (Connection Manager, \c SOURCE_ROUTE) that forwards elsewhere is not detected.

    @section ora_sandbox_connections_refused Connections that are Refused

    - \c TCPS (and <tt>tcps://</tt>): the certificate of the server is checked against the host name, which the
      checked numeric address would replace (see module-oracle issue 104)
    - other protocols than TCP (\c IPC, \c BEQ)
    - connections through an HTTPS proxy (\c HTTPS_PROXY)
    - Easy Connect parameters (<tt>?...</tt>) and Easy Connect strings without a service

    @since oracle 3.4.1
*/
